y have the high cards to beat a pair or single. Playing the Red Joker now is just handing them the advantage. This is not the winning path. I can only think of a miracle to win this trick.\n\nI\'ve got it. Pass. Pass and wait. It\'s the only smart play.\n\n**Evaluation:** Playing the Red Joker now guarantees control but leaves me with a weak hand that is likely to lose. Passing allows me to retain the Red Joker as a powerful defensive tool and to wait for a better opportunity.\n\n**Reason:**  Giving up the Red Joker too early puts me at a massive disadvantage. Holding it gives me a better chance to disrupt my opponents\' strategies and to potentially win the game in the long run. By passing, I am choosing the "slow and steady" way to a better play later. It is a long game, and the Red Joker is the best card left.\n\n**Answer:** {"cards": []}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current hand consists of `9, 10, 10, J, RedJoker`. The opponent (Previous Player) played a `BlackJoker`.\nOption 1: Beat with `RedJoker`. This wins the trick immediately, as the Red Joker is the highest card. However, this leaves a hand of `9, 10, 10, J` (a mid-rank pair and two unconnected singles). You would then have to lead. Leading a pair of 10s or a single 9/J against two opponents with 15 cards each is very risky; they likely have higher pairs (J, Q, K, A, 2) or singles. You would lose control and likely the game.\nOption 2: `Pass`. By passing, you allow the Previous Player to win the trick. You retain the Red Joker, which guarantees you can win a single-card trick later. You rely on the opponents to lead low singles or pairs, which gives you a chance to play your `9`, `J`, or `10s` without spending your ultimate control card to win a trick that gives you a disadvantageous lead.\n\nReason: Playing the Red Joker now forces you to lead with a very weak hand (pair of 10s or single 9) against opponents with large hands (15 cards). It is statistically probable they can beat your lead, leaving